narrative:
## Adding Conditions to Match Arms

**Match guards** add an extra condition (`if ...`) to a match arm. The arm only matches if both the pattern matches AND the guard condition is true.

### Basic Guard Syntax

```xiom
pub type NumberCategory = enum {
  Number(value: Int),
  Special,
}

fn categorize(n: &NumberCategory) -> Str {
  match n {
    Number(x) if x < 0 => { return "Negative"; }
    Number(x) if x == 0 => { return "Zero"; }
    Number(x) if x > 0 && x < 100 => { return "Small positive"; }
    Number(x) => { return "Large positive"; }
    Special => { return "Special"; }
  }
}
```

### Guard vs Separate Arms

Without guards, you'd need nested if/else inside the arm. Guards keep the logic flat and readable.

### Multiple Guards

Combine conditions with `&&` and `||`:

```xiom
pub type Score = enum {
  Points(value: Int),
  Absent,
}

fn grade(s: &Score) -> Str {
  match s {
    Points(v) if v >= 90 => { return "A"; }
    Points(v) if v >= 80 => { return "B"; }
    Points(v) if v >= 70 && v < 80 => { return "C"; }
    Points(v) => { return "F"; }
    Absent => { return "No grade"; }
  }
}
```

### Guards in Practice

```xiom
pub type Event = enum {
  KeyPress(code: Int),
  MouseClick,
}

fn is_navigation(key: &Event) -> Bool {
  match key {
    KeyPress(c) if c == 37 || c == 38 || c == 39 || c == 40 => {
      return true;  // arrow keys
    }
    KeyPress(c) if c == 13 => {
      return true;  // enter
    }
    _ => { return false; }
  }
}
```

### Guard Evaluation Order

Guards are evaluated from top to bottom. The first matching arm wins — order matters!

